{"parentUuid":"172be23b-4d5c-4fc1-b7a1-cf3b75e98b8b","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"deferred_tools_record","entries":[{"name":"EnterWorktree","description":"Use this tool ONLY when explicitly instructed to work in a worktree — either by the user directly, or by project instructions (CLAUDE.md / memory). This tool creates an isolated git worktree and switches the current session into it.\n\n## When to Use\n\n- The user explicitly says \"worktree\" (e.g., \"start a worktree\", \"work in a worktree\", \"create a worktree\", \"use a worktree\")\n- CLAUDE.md or memory instructions direct you to work in a worktree for the current task\n\n## When NOT to Use\n\n- The user asks to create a branch, switch branches, or work on a different branch — use git commands instead\n- The user asks to fix a bug or work on a feature — use normal git workflow unless worktrees are explicitly requested by the user or project instructions\n- Never use this tool unless \"worktree\" is explicitly mentioned by the user or in CLAUDE.md / memory instructions\n\n## Requirements\n\n- Must be in a git repository, OR have WorktreeCreate/WorktreeRemove hooks configured in settings.json\n- Must not already be in a worktree session when creating a new worktree (`name`); switching into another existing worktree via `path` is allowed\n\n## Behavior\n\n- In a git repository: creates a new git worktree inside `.claude/worktrees/` on a new branch. The base ref is governed by the `worktree.baseRef` setting: `fresh` (default) branches from origin/<default-branch>; `head` branches from your current local HEAD\n- Outside a git repository: delegates to WorktreeCreate/WorktreeRemove hooks for VCS-agnostic isolation\n- Switches the session's working directory to the new worktree\n- Use ExitWorktree to leave the worktree mid-session (keep or remove). On session exit, if still in the worktree, the user will be prompted to keep or remove it\n\n## Entering an existing worktree\n\nPass `path` instead of `name` to switch the session into a worktree that already exists (e.g., one you just created with `git worktree add`). On first entry from the launch directory, the path must appear in `git worktree list` for the repository that owns it — the current repository or, in a multi-repo workspace, a repository nested inside it; paths registered by neither are rejected. ExitWorktree will not remove a worktree entered this way; use `action: \"keep\"` to return to the original directory.\n\nSwitching with `path` also works when the session is already in a worktree (the previous worktree is left on disk, untouched, and only the new one is tracked for exit-time cleanup), and from agents whose working directory was pinned at launch (subagent isolation or explicit cwd). In both cases the target must be a worktree under `.claude/worktrees/` of the same repository, and from a pinned agent the switch only affects this agent, not the parent session. After a further switch, previously-visited worktrees are no longer writable — re-issue EnterWorktree with `path` to return to one.\n\n## Parameters\n\n- `name` (optional): A name for a new worktree. If neither `name` nor `path` is provided, a random name is generated.\n- `path` (optional): Path to an existing worktree to enter instead of creating one — of the current repository, or (on first entry from the launch directory) of a repository nested inside it. Mutually exclusive with `name`.\n","input_schema":{"$schema":"https://json-schema.org/draft/2020-12/schema","type":"object","properties":{"name":{"description":"Optional name for a new worktree. Each \"/\"-separated segment may contain only letters, digits, dots, underscores, and dashes; max 64 chars total. A random name is generated if not provided. Mutually exclusive with `path`.","type":"string"},"path":{"description":"Path to an existing worktree to switch into instead of creating a new one. Must appear in `git worktree list` for the current repo — or, on first entry from the launch directory, for a repo nested inside it (multi-repo workspace). Mutually exclusive with `name`.","type":"string"}},"additionalProperties":false},"eager_input_streaming":true,"defer_loading":true},{"name":"WebFetch","description":"Fetches a URL, converts the page to markdown, and answers `prompt` against it using a small fast model.\n\n- Fails on authenticated/private URLs — use an authenticated MCP tool or `gh` for those instead. claude.ai artifact links (claude.ai/artifact/{id} or claude.ai/code/artifact/{uuid}) are published artifacts: read them with the Artifact tool (action \"read\"), not WebFetch or curl.\n- Fails on localhost and other hostnames without a dot; for a local server, use curl via Bash.\n- HTTP is upgraded to HTTPS. Cross-host redirects are returned to you rather than followed; call again with the redirect URL.\n- Responses are cached for 15 minutes per URL.","input_schema":{"$schema":"https://json-schema.org/draft/2020-12/schema","type":"object","properties":{"url":{"description":"The URL to fetch content from","type":"string","format":"uri"},"prompt":{"description":"The prompt to run on the fetched content","type":"string"},"offset":{"description":"Character position in the page text to start reading from. Use it to read on through a page too long for one call, with the value the previous result gave.","type":"integer","minimum":0,"maximum":9007199254740991}},"required":["url","prompt"],"additionalProperties":false},"eager_input_streaming":true,"defer_loading":true},{"name":"WebSearch","description":"Search the web. Returns result blocks with titles and URLs. US-only.\n\n- The current month is (provided in the conversation below) — use this when searching for recent information.\n- `allowed_domains` / `blocked_domains` filter results.\n- After answering from results, end with a \"Sources:\" list of the URLs you used as markdown links.","input_schema":{"$schema":"https://json-schema.org/draft/2020-12/schema","type":"object","properties":{"query":{"description":"The search query to use","type":"string","minLength":2},"allowed_domains":{"description":"Only include search results from these domains","type":"array","items":{"type":"string"}},"blocked_domains":{"description":"Never include search results from these domains","type":"array","items":{"type":"string"}},"mode":{"description":"\"standard\": the normal web search: quick and cheap; right for straightforward lookups (reference facts, official pages, documentation, well-known people, places and topics) and simple follow-up lookups. \"extended\": a thorough, fresh search at several times the cost and latency.","type":"string","enum":["standard","extended"]}},"required":["query","mode"],"additionalProperties":false},"eager_input_streaming":true,"defer_loading":true},{"name":"mcp__20ba8580-bd26-489b-9d1c-034028af6897__get_episode","description":"Everything about one episode: show notes, chapters, guests, the sponsor read, every link (platform, format, URL, status, when it went out, who posted it, files used) and its files with download URLs valid for an hour. Give episodeId or number.","input_schema":{"type":"object","properties":{"podcast":{"description":"Podcast id (e.g. \"podcast\" for Entra.Chat); leave out when you edit only one podcast","type":"string"},"episodeId":{"description":"Episode id from list_episodes","type":"integer","minimum":-9007199254740991,"maximum":9007199254740991},"number":{"description":"Or the episode number, e.g. 79","type":"integer","minimum":-9007199254740991,"maximum":9007199254740991}},"$schema":"https://json-schema.org/draft/2020-12/schema"},"eager_input_streaming":true,"defer_loading":true},{"name":"mcp__20ba8580-bd26-489b-9d1c-034028af6897__list_episodes","description":"A podcast’s episodes (Entra.Chat by default), newest first: number, title, publish date, guests, platforms it is published on, whether the full YouTube video is linked, links needing review, and how many files it has.","input_schema":{"type":"object","properties":{"podcast":{"description":"Podcast id (e.g. \"podcast\" for Entra.Chat); leave out when you edit only one podcast","type":"string"},"limit":{"default":20,"type":"integer","minimum":1,"maximum":200},"offset":{"default":0,"type":"integer","minimum":0,"maximum":9007199254740991}},"$schema":"https://json-schema.org/draft/2020-12/schema"},"eager_input_streaming":true,"defer_loading":true},{"name":"mcp__c39b39a8-1877-4f58-88b0-3495605b1158__platform_get_transcript","description":"Get the transcript of a recording by session id. For uploads and single-take recordings, use `id` as `sessionId`; the same value feeds `editing_create_edit_from_recording`. This returns dialogue prose, each line headed by `Speaker (m:s.mmm)` — one timestamp per sentence, measured from the start of the recording session. Note minutes and seconds are not zero-padded (`1:5.000` is 1m5s). Exception: when a recording exposes no offset for a speaker, that speaker is timed from their own track start instead, so their times are not comparable with the other speakers. For word-level times, or for cutting, use `editing_read_aligned_transcript`.","input_schema":{"type":"object","properties":{"sessionId":{"type":"string","description":"The session id of the recording to get the transcript for; for uploads and single-take recordings, use the recording `id`."}},"required":["sessionId"],"$schema":"http://json-schema.org/draft-07/schema#"},"eager_input_streaming":true,"defer_loading":true},{"name":"REDACTED","description":"Exact-phrase search over recording/take transcript content across the caller's archive (higher precision than search_riverside's fuzzy matching). Returns compact per-session matches with sessionId, takeId, projectId and up to maxHighlightsPerSession ~200-character transcript fragments, with no word timestamps. Feed sessionId, projectId, and a title to editing_create_edit_from_recording, or use platform_get_transcript for prose. sessionId identifies the recording session rather than the individual take, so for a multi-take recording it may resolve to a sibling take: confirm the intended take with the user first, or start from platform_list_edits if the recording already has an edit. TAKES/recordings only — clip and project transcripts are not searched.","input_schema":{"type":"object","properties":{"searchTerms":{"minItems":1,"maxItems":20,"type":"array","items":{"type":"string","minLength":1,"maxLength":256},"description":"Exact phrases matched verbatim against recording transcripts; multiple phrases are OR-combined."},"size":{"description":"Maximum number of sessions to return. Defaults to 20 when omitted.","type":"integer","minimum":1,"maximum":50},"createdAfterDate":{"description":"Only recordings created after this date. Defaults to the last 120 days when omitted.","anyOf":[{"type":"string","format":"date-time","pattern":"^(?:(?:\\d\\d[2468][048]|\\d\\d[13579][26]|\\d\\d0[48]|[02468][048]00|[13579][26]00)-02-29|\\d{4}-(?:(?:0[13578]|1[02])-(?:0[1-9]|[12]\\d|3[01])|(?:0[469]|11)-(?:0[1-9]|[12]\\d|30)|(?:02)-(?:0[1-9]|1\\d|2[0-8])))T(?:(?:[01]\\d|2[0-3]):[0-5]\\d(?::[0-5]\\d(?:\\.\\d+)?)?(?:Z))$"},{"type":"string","format":"date","pattern":"^(?:(?:\\d\\d[2468][048]|\\d\\d[13579][26]|\\d\\d0[48]|[02468][048]00|[13579][26]00)-02-29|\\d{4}-(?:(?:0[13578]|1[02])-(?:0[1-9]|[12]\\d|3[01])|(?:0[469]|11)-(?:0[1-9]|[12]\\d|30)|(?:02)-(?:0[1-9]|1\\d|2[0-8])))$"}]},"maxHighlightsPerSession":{"description":"Maximum transcript fragments returned per session. Defaults to 10 when omitted.","type":"integer","minimum":1,"maximum":20}},"required":["searchTerms"],"$schema":"http://json-schema.org/draft-07/schema#"},"eager_input_streaming":true,"defer_loading":true},{"name":"mcp__c39b39a8-1877-4f58-88b0-3495605b1158__search_riverside","description":"Search Riverside projects, clips, and recording takes with fuzzy matching, including spoken-word transcript content for takes. Use search_recording_transcripts_exact when the wording must match exactly; that tool has no duration-range parameter. For spoken moments, pass a filters entry whose entity is TAKE. Transcript/content matching applies only to takes/recordings. Projects and clips match by title; studios and productions are not searchable entities. When the user requests a duration, include durationRange in the matching CLIP or TAKE filter before pagination, with inclusive min, max, or both in seconds. A range applies to each matching entity, not the total duration of the results; PROJECT does not support a non-null range. Keep field and value present, using null when unused, and preserve requested project/production scope. Results expose compact public fields, identifiers, and short transcript fragments in highlights.transcription, with no word timestamps and no full transcript. Result duration values retain their source units: CLIP milliseconds and TAKE seconds. No tool has a parameter named takeId or entityId; takeId is display-only, while the entityId on a CLIP result is the clip ID that social_upload_create accepts as clipId. Global search locates a TAKE result. To edit it, call editing_create_edit_from_recording(sessionId, projectId, title) to create a new editable timeline, then editing_read_aligned_transcript(editId) for word-level times; platform_get_transcript(sessionId) returns dialogue prose instead. A take's sessionId identifies its recording session, not the individual take, so for a multi-take recording it may resolve to a sibling take: confirm the intended take with the user first, or start from platform_list_edits if the recording already has an edit. Results page 10 at a time via nextSearchAfter; there is no limit parameter. Repeat the same search term and filters, including durationRange and scope selectors, while paging. Filtering only the first page locally is incomplete. Pass explicit null for unused nullable parameters.","input_schema":{"type":"object","properties":{"searchTerm":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string"},{"type":"null"}],"description":"Required but nullable. Search phrase or topic; pass explicit null when unused."},"filters":{"anyOf":[{"type":"array","items":{"type":"object","properties":{"field":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string"},{"type":"null"}],"description":"Required but nullable. Field name to filter on (e.g., \"type\" or \"project_id\"); pass null when unused."},"value":{"anyOf":[{"type":"array","items":{"type":"string"}},{"type":"null"}],"description":"Required but nullable. Values to match; pass null when unused."},"entity":{"type":"string","enum":["PROJECT","TAKE","CLIP"],"description":"Entity filter type. Accepts only PROJECT, TAKE, or CLIP."},"durationRange":{"description":"Optional duration filter for each matching CLIP or TAKE, applied before pagination. Supply min, max, or both in seconds, with at least one numeric bound and min <= max. Null or omission means no duration filter, including for PROJECT.","anyOf":[{"type":"object","properties":{"min":{"description":"Inclusive minimum duration in seconds; null or omission leaves the minimum unbounded.","anyOf":[{"type":"number","minimum":0},{"type":"null"}]},"max":{"description":"Inclusive maximum duration in seconds; null or omission leaves the maximum unbounded.","anyOf":[{"type":"number","minimum":0},{"type":"null"}]}}},{"type":"null"}]}},"required":["field","value","entity"]}},{"type":"null"}],"description":"Required but nullable. Pass explicit null for no filters. Entity filters accept only PROJECT, TAKE, or CLIP; use TAKE for transcript matches. Apply requested durationRange before pagination, preserve requested scope selectors, and repeat all filters on subsequent pages."},"nextSearchAfter":{"anyOf":[{"minItems":3,"maxItems":3,"type":"array","items":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string"},{"type":"number"},{"type":"null"}]}},{"type":"null"}],"description":"Required but nullable. Pass explicit null for the first page, then pass nextSearchAfter from the prior response to fetch the next 10 results. Repeat the same search term and filters, including durationRange and scope selectors."}},"required":["searchTerm","filters","nextSearchAfter"],"$schema":"http://json-schema.org/draft-07/schema#"},"eager_input_streaming":true,"defer_loading":true},{"name":"mcp__ccd_host__sync_with_base_branch","description":"Bring this session's branch up to date with its base branch: the app fetches the base from `origin` and merges it into the branch checked out in this session's worktree, running git on the host, outside your sandbox, which cannot write the repository's protected paths (`.claude/hooks`, `.claude/skills`, `.mcp.json` and the like) that such a merge often touches. Use it instead of `git merge`/`git pull` whenever you need the base branch's latest commits, e.g. to resolve a pull request's merge conflicts.\n\n`base` defaults to the base the app knows for this session (its open pull request's base, else the branch the worktree was cut from); you may name that branch or the repository's default branch, nothing else. A session that also holds a worktree for an extra folder of another repository merges into the worktree your current directory is in, or the one `folder` names (either path the workspace note shows for that folder, or the folder's last segment when only one of the session's folders ends in it); the base is then that repository's. Sandbox-protected files are brought in only from the repository's default branch; any other base merges only when it leaves those files alone, and a protected file both this branch and the base changed is left for a person (the call refuses). Nothing is pushed. Outcomes: merged (a merge commit or fast-forward; run the project's checks, then push), up to date, or conflicts: the merge is then left in progress with the conflicted files listed for you to resolve, `git add` and `git commit --no-edit` (protected files this branch only had an older upstream copy of are already taken from the base). `abort: true` abandons a merge left in progress instead. It refuses rather than touch uncommitted changes, another git operation in progress, files the repository routes through a content filter (git-lfs, git-crypt), a fork checkout whose origin is not the pull request's repository, or a session without its own worktree; merge yourself in those cases. In default mode the user approves each call on a card; in auto mode the app's classifier decides.","input_schema":{"type":"object","properties":{"base":{"description":"The base branch to merge, when the session's own isn't the one you need: its pull request's base, the branch the worktree was cut from, or the repository's default branch.","type":"string"},"abort":{"description":"true to abandon a merge this tool left in progress (git merge --abort) instead of merging.","type":"boolean"},"folder":{"description":"Which of this session's worktrees to act on, when it holds one for an extra folder of another repository: either path the workspace note shows for that folder, or its last segment when only one of the session's folders ends in it; the session's own folder names its own worktree. Default: the worktree your current directory is in, else the session's own.","type":"string"},"_consent":{"description":"Set by the app. Never set this yourself.","type":"string"}},"$schema":"http://json-schema.org/draft-07/schema#"},"eager_input_streaming":true,"defer_loading":true},{"name":"mcp__ccd_pr__bind_pr","description":"Bind this session to a PR the PR bar did not pick up (fork remote, GitHub Enterprise host, PR opened elsewhere) by its URL. The repository must be this checkout's origin or one the user's sessions already have a PR bound from. Restores a dismissed open PR. The user is asked to approve (in auto mode the app may approve without asking, judging from the conversation, except to restore a PR the user dismissed; in bypass permissions mode it does not ask), so bind only a PR the user asked you to track.","input_schema":{"type":"object","properties":{"url":{"description":"The pull request URL, e.g. https://github.com/owner/repo/pull/123.","type":"string"},"_consent":{"description":"Set by the app. Do not set it.","type":"string"}},"required":["url"],"$schema":"http://json-schema.org/draft-07/schema#"},"eager_input_streaming":true,"defer_loading":true},{"name":"mcp__ccd_pr__get_status","description":"Read the pull request bound to a Code session in this app (default \"self\") and its CI monitor: PR number/url/state/branches, CI check counts and failing check names, review decision, mergeability, and the auto_fix / auto_merge / auto_archive_on_close switches. Served from the app's cache. Use after `gh pr create` instead of polling `gh pr checks`.","input_schema":{"type":"object","properties":{"session_id":{"description":"The session to read: the literal string \"self\" (default) or a sessionId from ccd_session_mgmt list_sessions.","type":"string"}},"$schema":"http://json-schema.org/draft-07/schema#"},"eager_input_streaming":true,"defer_loading":true},{"name":"mcp__ccd_pr__set_monitor","description":"Set this session's CI monitor switches for its bound, open PR. auto_fix: the app wakes this session with a `<ci-monitor-event>` on CI failures, merge conflicts and review comments; turn it on only for a PR this session opened. address_comments must equal auto_fix on this machine. auto_archive_on_close: archive this session once the PR merges or closes. Pass only the switches to change, and the PR's url with auto_fix. The user is asked to approve each call (in auto mode the app may approve without asking, and in bypass permissions mode it does not ask), so only call it when the user asked for that switch.","input_schema":{"type":"object","properties":{"auto_fix":{"description":"Wake this session on CI failures, merge conflicts and review comments.","type":"boolean"},"address_comments":{"description":"Review-comment delivery. On this machine it is the same switch as auto_fix and must equal it when passed.","type":"boolean"},"auto_archive_on_close":{"description":"Archive this session once its PR merges or closes.","type":"boolean"},"url":{"description":"The bound pull request's URL, as get_status reports it. Required with auto_fix.","type":"string"}},"$schema":"http://json-schema.org/draft-07/schema#"},"eager_input_streaming":true,"defer_loading":true},{"name":"mcp__ccd_view__show_pane","description":"Show one of this session's side panes in the user's view of the Claude desktop app, beside the conversation.\n\nPanes: \"diff\" (the session's changes; optional `path` scrolls to that file, `diff_scope` picks all branch changes, uncommitted changes only, or one commit), \"file\" (`path` opens that file in the Files pane at `line`; must be inside the session's working directory or a folder the user granted), \"terminal\" (optional `tab_id` switches to that open tab), \"pr\" (the session's pull request), \"tasks\" (background tasks), \"plan\", \"preview\" (the Browser pane, once this session has a page open in it), \"artifact\" (the artifacts this session published). A page in a hidden Browser pane runs as a background page: animation may not run at all, and on some systems timers slow down once you haven't touched the page for a while. Showing the pane with that page's tab in front helps only while the Claude window is itself in view (not minimized or covered by another app), so don't rely on timing or performance numbers from the page unless the user is watching it.\n\nPrefer showing over describing: after finishing a set of edits, show the diff; when pointing the user at code, open the file at the line. It only changes what is on screen for this session — if the session isn't open in any window it does nothing and says so (tell the user what to look at instead), and it never takes focus from another session. A pane in a window of its own is left where it is, never raised, reopened here or changed, and the result says so: tell the user to look there.","input_schema":{"type":"object","properties":{"pane":{"type":"string","enum":["diff","file","terminal","pr","tasks","plan","artifact","preview"]},"path":{"description":"For \"file\": the file to open (absolute, or relative to the working directory). For \"diff\": the changed file to scroll to.","type":"string"},"line":{"type":"number","description":"For \"file\": 1-based line to scroll to."},"diff_scope":{"description":"For \"diff\": \"all\" (branch changes, the default), \"uncommitted\", or a commit SHA.","type":"string"},"tab_id":{"description":"For \"terminal\": the open tab to switch to, by the tab_id a terminal tool or an attached terminal snippet gave you. Omit to show the terminal as it is.","type":"string"}},"required":["pane"],"$schema":"http://json-schema.org/draft-07/schema#"},"eager_input_streaming":true,"defer_loading":true},{"name":"mcp__nucleo__get_icon_svg","description":"Get the raw SVG source of a Nucleo icon by ID.","input_schema":{"type":"object","properties":{"icon_id":{"type":"number","description":"The icon ID"}},"required":["icon_id"]},"eager_input_streaming":true,"defer_loading":true},{"name":"mcp__nucleo__get_icon_variants","description":"Get all style variants of an icon by name (outline, solid, duotone-outline, duotone-solid), with previews. Use this to find the right variant to match an existing icon's visual style.","input_schema":{"type":"object","properties":{"name":{"type":"string","description":"Exact icon name (e.g. 'alarm-clock')"},"set_id":{"type":"number","description":"Limit to a specific set ID (optional)"},"preview":{"type":"boolean","description":"Include PNG preview images (default: true)"}},"required":["name"]},"eager_input_streaming":true,"defer_loading":true},{"name":"mcp__nucleo__search_icons","description":"Search Nucleo icons by name and/or tags. Returns matching icons with metadata.","input_schema":{"type":"object","properties":{"query":{"type":"string","description":"Search term to match against icon names and tags"},"limit":{"type":"number","description":"Max results to return (default: 20, max: 100)"},"set_id":{"type":"number","description":"Filter by icon set ID (optional)"},"favorites_only":{"type":"boolean","description":"Only return favorited icons (optional)"},"variant":{"type":"string","enum":["outline","solid","duotone-outline","duotone-solid"],"description":"Filter by visual style variant (optional)"}},"required":["query"]},"eager_input_streaming":true,"defer_loading":true},{"name":"mcp__reui__get_component","description":"Fetch one or MORE ReUI components by name with their INLINE API (props + usage, as markdown) so you can wire them WITHOUT a web fetch, plus a 1-line digest of the required props, install command, dependencies, previewUrl (its live component page), and docsUrl (the component's API documentation page - SHARE it with the user when working with the component's API, so they have the full reference). Pass a single name (e.g. 'data-grid') or an ARRAY of names to read several APIs in ONE call - prefer the array to avoid round-trips. A very large API is trimmed on heading boundaries to fit your context and everything dropped is listed in sectionsOmitted; ask for exactly what you need with `sections` (e.g. sections: ['DataGridPagination']) or raise `maxChars`. After reading the API, call get_examples(name) to copy a worked example's exact composition. A shadcn/ui component ReUI has examples for (e.g. 'select', 'hover-card') answers kind 'shadcn' with api null, never an invented one: its shadcn install command, where its API really lives (externalDocsUrl) and ReUI's examples built on it. file-upload answers kind 'reui-hook' with the documented API of the hook it ships as.","input_schema":{"$schema":"http://json-schema.org/draft-07/schema#","type":"object","properties":{"name":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string","maxLength":1680},{"minItems":1,"maxItems":20,"type":"array","items":{"type":"string","maxLength":80}}],"description":"A component name, or an array of names."},"sections":{"description":"Heading names from the API to return instead of the whole capsule, case-insensitive (e.g. ['DataGridPagination']). A parent heading brings its sub-sections with it. Returned whole unless you also set maxChars.","maxItems":20,"type":"array","items":{"type":"string","maxLength":80}},"maxChars":{"description":"Per-component character cap for the API markdown. Default 30000; trimming happens on heading boundaries, never mid table.","type":"integer","minimum":4000,"maximum":200000}},"required":["name"]},"eager_input_streaming":true,"defer_loading":true},{"name":"mcp__reui__get_thumbnail","description":"Fetch the hosted preview IMAGE of a block, example or icon as image content, so YOU can see it before installing. Call this only when the choice is genuinely VISUAL and the ranking has not already made it: 2 or more candidates within about 15 score points, or compose_page alternates you are weighing. When the top hit leads by a wide margin, or its `missedTerms` already rule the others out, skip this and save the tokens - an image costs roughly 10 times a result row. Pass up to 4 names in ONE call to compare side by side and pick by looking instead of by score. Each image is followed by a text block naming the item and its previewUrl and docsUrl. To let the USER see an item, share the `thumbnail` and `previewUrl` already on that item's result instead; this tool is for your own eyes. An icon's image shows its 4 styles side by side (640x200), so you can check the drawing matches what you need before installing it; an old icon name answers with the icon it became. Components have no preview image and come back as text. Free, no license needed.","input_schema":{"$schema":"http://json-schema.org/draft-07/schema#","type":"object","properties":{"name":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string","maxLength":336},{"minItems":1,"maxItems":4,"type":"array","items":{"type":"string","maxLength":80}}],"description":"A block, example or icon name, or an array of up to 4 to compare."},"theme":{"description":"Which capture to fetch. Default light; an item that renders identically in both themes always serves light.","type":"string","enum":["light","dark"]}},"required":["name"]},"eager_input_streaming":true,"defer_loading":true},{"name":"mcp__reui__search","description":"Start here for any ReUI or shadcn UI request. ReUI workflow: search -> get_component to read the real API before writing props -> get_install_command and install with the shadcn CLI -> adapt by REUSE (wire real data and theme; never hand-roll or restyle what ReUI already provides) -> validate_usage. Find the best ReUI items for a natural-language query across four types: components (the 27 ReUI building blocks with real APIs, e.g. data-grid, kanban), examples (free c-* single-pattern use-cases), blocks (premium full-page sections), and icons (premium, 4 styles each; only some have a hover-animated variant, flagged `animated`). Returns a ranked list; each result carries its install command, a docsUrl pointing at that item's own page, a LIVE previewUrl (a hosted preview page), a thumbnail image URL for blocks, examples and icons, componentsUsed, dependencies, free flag (premium items say requiredPlan), a relative score (0-100 vs the top hit), and termCoverage (0-1 share of the query matched). SHOW the user the thumbnail as a markdown image linked to previewUrl so they SEE the item before installing - this is especially valuable for free components and c-* examples, which they can preview and use immediately. A top-level componentDigests map carries a compact API contract per referenced component. Pass structured hints (type/component/category/free) when you can infer them; a hint the server could not use comes back under ignoredHints instead of being silently dropped.","input_schema":{"$schema":"http://json-schema.org/draft-07/schema#","type":"object","properties":{"query":{"type":"string","maxLength":500,"description":"What the user wants, e.g. 'data-grid for audit logs'"},"type":{"type":"string","enum":["block","component","example","icon"]},"component":{"description":"A ReUI component to bias toward, e.g. 'data-grid'","type":"string"},"category":{"description":"Restrict to a category slug","type":"string"},"features":{"type":"array","items":{"type":"string"}},"free":{"type":"boolean"},"surface":{"description":"Lock results to ONE design-system surface so blocks stay visually consistent. 'frame' = ReUI Frame blocks, 'card' = shadcn Card blocks; surface-neutral blocks (heroes, shells, auth) are kept in both, and internally-mixed blocks are excluded. Pick one surface per project and pass it on every search.","type":"string","enum":["frame","card"]},"limit":{"type":"integer","minimum":1,"maximum":25}},"required":["query"]},"eager_input_streaming":true,"defer_loading":true}]},"type":"attachment","uuid":"8ffec6ed-3e8e-45a7-8b6f-9c424e7de585","timestamp":"2026-11-02T21:27:06.746Z","userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-desktop","cwd":"/Users/merill/github/maester/.claude/worktrees/website-astro","sessionId":"2d9f0177-c129-494c-a54f-610e3d37d77a","version":"2.1.309","gitBranch":"feat/website-astro","slug":"breezy-giggling-flame"}
